.Dd September 20, 2025
.Dt R_FS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm r_fs
.Nd radare2 filesystem library
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In r_fs.h
.Pp
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm r_fs
library provides filesystem support for radare2, allowing analysis and manipulation of various filesystem types within binary images. It supports mounting, reading, writing, and navigating filesystems embedded in disk images or memory dumps.
.Pp
Typical usage in the radare2 core allocates a filesystem context with
.Ft RFS *
.Va fs
via
.Fn r_fs_new "void",
and mounts a filesystem with
.Fn r_fs_mount "RFS *fs" "const char *fstype" "const char *path" "ut64 delta",
so that commands in the core can list directories, open files and read their contents through the unified API. The core keeps a reference to the returned
.Vt RFS
context (see `libr/core/core.c`), and calls
.Fn r_fs_free "RFS *fs"
when tearing down.
.Pp
The core structure is
.Vt RFS ,
which manages plugins, mounted roots and binds I/O and console operations (see `libr/include/r_fs.h` for fields and types).
.Sh INITIALIZATION
.Ft RFS *
.Fn r_fs_new "void"
.Pp
Creates a new filesystem context with built-in plugins loaded.
.Pp
.Ft void
.Fn r_fs_free "RFS *fs"
.Pp
Frees all resources associated with the filesystem context.
.Sh MOUNTING
.Ft RFSRoot *
.Fn r_fs_mount "RFS *fs" "const char *fstype" "const char *path" "ut64 delta"
.Pp
Attachs a filesystem implementation (plugin) to a virtual mount point. The `delta` argument is commonly used when the filesystem is found at an offset inside a disk image or memory buffer. In the radare2 core this is typically invoked as:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
RFS *fs = r_fs_new();
RFSRoot *root = r_fs_mount (fs, "auto", "/root", core->addr /* offset */);
.Ed
.Pp
.Ft bool
.Fn r_fs_umount "RFS *fs" "const char *path"
.Pp
Unmounts the filesystem at the specified path.
.Sh FILE OPERATIONS
.Ft RFSFile *
.Fn r_fs_open "RFS *fs" "const char *path" "bool create"
.Pp
Open a file relative to mounted roots; when multiple roots match the path the first matching root is used. Callers in the core often do:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
RFSFile *f = r_fs_open (core->fs, "/some/path", false);
if (f) {
    r_fs_read (core->fs, f, 0, f->size);
    r_fs_close (core->fs, f);
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Ft void
.Fn r_fs_close "RFS *fs" "RFSFile *file"
.Pp
Closes an open file handle.
.Pp
.Ft int
.Fn r_fs_read "RFS *fs" "RFSFile *file" "ut64 addr" "int len"
.Pp
Reads data from a file at the specified 64-bit offset. File sizes are ut64;
each read request and its return value remain int-sized. Read large files in
bounded chunks. RFSFile.data contains the most recent read, while RFSFile.size
retains the full logical file size.
.Pp
.Ft int
.Fn r_fs_write "RFS *fs" "RFSFile *file" "ut64 addr" "const ut8 *data" "int len"
.Pp
Writes data to a file at the specified offset.
.Sh DIRECTORY OPERATIONS
.Ft RList *
.Fn r_fs_dir "RFS *fs" "const char *path" "ut64 id" "int view"
.Pp
Returns a `RList` of `RFSFile` objects for the given virtual directory. The core uses this to implement the `md` command and directory listings; for example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
RList *files = r_fs_dir (core->fs, "/", UT64_MAX, R_FS_VIEW_NORMAL);
// iterate and inspect file->name, file->size, file->type
.Ed
.Pp
FAT directory listings retain entries read before corruption and log a warning.
A directory that cannot be read at all returns NULL.
.Pp
.Ft bool
.Fn r_fs_dir_dump "RFS *fs" "const char *path" "const char *name"
.Pp
Dumps the contents of a directory to disk.
.Sh FILE SLURPING
.Ft RFSFile *
.Fn r_fs_slurp "RFS *fs" "const char *path"
.Pp
Convenience helper that opens and reads a file fully into memory returning an `RFSFile` whose `data` and `size` fields are populated. Files larger than INT_MAX are rejected; use bounded reads or streaming extraction for those files. This is useful when a single buffer is required by consumers (e.g. analysis or dumping).
.Sh SEARCHING
.Ft RList *
.Fn r_fs_find_name "RFS *fs" "const char *name" "const char *glob" "int view"
.Pp
Finds files by name using glob patterns.
.Pp
.Ft RList *
.Fn r_fs_find_off "RFS *fs" "const char *name" "ut64 off" "int view"
.Pp
Finds files containing data at a specific offset.
.Sh PARTITIONS
.Ft RList *
.Fn r_fs_partitions "RFS *fs" "const char *ptype" "ut64 delta"
.Pp
Enumerates partitions using the built-in partition detectors or a specific partition type. The returned list contains `RFSPartition` entries describing start and length; callers often use this to discover filesystems inside disk images before mounting them.
.Sh PLUGINS
.Ft bool
.Fn r_fs_plugin_add "RFS *fs" "RFSPlugin *p"
.Pp
Adds a filesystem plugin to the context.
.Pp
.Sh FORENSIC OPERATIONS
.Ft RFSFile *
.Fn r_fs_open_id "RFS *fs" "const char *mountpoint" "ut64 id" "ut32 flags"
.Pp
Open a metadata entry by its filesystem-specific ID in an exact mounted root.
FAT IDs are volume-relative byte offsets of short directory entries.
Deleted short names use an underscore for the missing first byte. Valid
adjacent VFAT fragments can supply a long name, marked R_FS_FILE_HEURISTIC;
the checksum is evidence of association, not proof of historical identity.
Opening by ID can reconstruct names within the containing directory cluster
or fixed root region; fragments in an earlier noncontiguous cluster require
normal directory enumeration. OEM bytes without a known codepage are
rendered as hexadecimal escapes.
R_FS_OPEN_CHAIN follows surviving FAT links. R_FS_OPEN_CONTIGUOUS is an
explicit heuristic for deleted regular files and only uses consecutive free
clusters. It never skips allocated clusters to assemble a guess.
The returned handle uses the ordinary read, close and file-free operations.
Extent enumeration normally reports the mappings claimed by directory
metadata, including deleted claims whose clusters may now be reused.
R_FS_OPEN_RECOVER requests ownership-checked recovery extents;
R_FS_OPEN_CONTIGUOUS implies it. Deleted data reads always check live ownership.
R_FS_OPEN_SALVAGE, combined with R_FS_OPEN_RECOVER, makes ext2 and NTFS extent
enumeration skip reused or unknown storage and continue with later retained
mappings instead of stopping; the walk still reports R_FS_WALK_ERROR and the
readable prefix is unchanged. FAT accepts and ignores it because links past a
reused cluster belong to the new owner.
Recovery previews and extraction commands use ownership-checked extents. Incomplete
ownership enumeration refuses recovery; reused clusters stop the readable
prefix and set R_FS_FILE_CONFLICT. R_FS_FILE_PARTIAL records incomplete data.
The declared size remains unchanged; reads can return a short prefix, then an
error. No missing content is synthesized. Open a new handle after modifying
the image, because the recovery plan is retained for that handle.
The ID and disk offset use UT64_MAX when unknown; deletion is recorded in
RFSFile.flags independently of the file type.
.Pp
Directory listing and searches take an explicit visibility mask:
R_FS_VIEW_NORMAL, R_FS_VIEW_DELETED, R_FS_VIEW_SPECIAL or R_FS_VIEW_ALL.
For directory listing, UT64_MAX selects the directory by path; another ID
selects directory metadata in the filesystem mounted above that path.
.Pp
.Ft int
.Fn r_fs_extents "RFSFile *file" "RFSRangeCb cb" "void *user"
.Pp
Enumerate logical file offsets and their physical backing-image locations.
Ranges exclude file slack; contiguous logical and physical runs are coalesced.
R_FS_RANGE_SPARSE marks a hole that reads as zero and has no physical backing;
its physical offset is UT64_MAX. R_FS_RANGE_UNWRITTEN marks allocated storage
that reads as zero; its physical offset identifies the allocated disk range.
Unwritten ranges retain current allocation flags where available. These flags describe logical
file contents and do not establish that the physical bytes contain zeros.
For FAT, allocation flags describe the current FAT state of the mapped clusters.
For classic ext2, IDs are inode numbers. Deleted directory names are inferred
from directory slack and marked heuristic. Listing deleted entries with
directory ID 0 scans free inodes and assigns synthetic inode-N names, allowing
discovery when no directory name survives. Recovery checks both allocation
bitmaps and live inode references, including filesystem metadata. It stops at
reused or unreadable storage and retains the known prefix. Cleared inode block
pointers cannot establish the original locations of deleted data.
Explicit contiguous recovery guesses consecutive blocks starting at the first
retained data pointer, applies the same ownership checks, and marks the result
heuristic. Sparse recovery requires the retained block map to account for the
inode's recorded block count; otherwise missing pointers stop the known prefix.
Recovering a symlink writes its target text to the output file.
Typical commands after mounting an ext2 image at /root are:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mdx /root             # deleted directory names and inode IDs
mdx /root 0           # carve free inodes without names
mie /root 13          # inspect inode 13's retained block map
mgx /root 13 output   # recover inode 13 with ownership checks
mfu /root free        # unallocated block ranges
mfu /root orphan      # allocated blocks without live owners
.Ed
A damaged or cleared chain can yield a known prefix followed by WALK_ERROR;
this is not a complete recovery.
.Pp
For NTFS, IDs are MFT record numbers. The reader discovers names from retained
FILE_NAME attributes, independently of directory indexes. Parent record sequence
numbers must match before a name is attached to a directory. Deleted entries
with missing names can be discovered with directory ID 0 as mft-N entries.
Resident data, fragmented nonresident mapping pairs, sparse runs and initialized
data lengths are supported. Resident extents identify the actual on-disk bytes,
including update-sequence-array words that restore sector trailers.
Resident and nonresident ATTRIBUTE_LIST values resolve attributes across MFT
extension records, checking sequence numbers, base references, attribute
instances, types, names and lowest VCNs. Continuation segments must form a
contiguous logical map. Fragmented MFT mappings are bootstrapped progressively
from reachable extension records. Incomplete mappings retain inspection of
readable records without permitting checked recovery or orphan classification.
.Pp
NTFS recovery requires a clear MFT allocation bit and an unused record. Nonresident
data must also have clear volume allocation bits and no live or metadata owner.
Nonresident attribute-list storage must pass the same checks; reused or unknown
list storage limits recovery to mappings retained in the base record. Resident
data in an extension record also requires that record's MFT allocation bit to
be clear. Missing or invalid extension references retain only validated prefixes.
Both in-use flags and MFT bitmap references protect live data, including named
nonresident attributes. Bad clusters come from the BadClus stream. Unreadable,
sparse or uninitialized allocation bitmaps produce unknown space, never free
space. Reused clusters stop recovery while retaining its readable prefix.
A free allocation bit does not prove that clusters were never overwritten and freed again.
.Pp
NTFS metadata is cached as a read-only mounted snapshot; unmount and remount
after modifying the backing image. Named DATA attributes appear alongside the
unnamed data stream in the virtual directory /mount/file/..namedfork.
The data entry reuses the MFT record ID; ads:NAME entries have snapshot IDs
with bit 62 set. Stream names are selected exactly as listed. Control characters,
percent, colon and path separators are escaped as %XX; unpaired UTF-16 surrogates
use %uXXXX. This preserves distinct stream identities without interpreting
colons in ordinary filenames. Full NTFS Unicode case equivalence remains
separate name-handling work.
.Pp
Existing commands accept stream paths, for example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mdj /root/live.txt/..namedfork
mc /root/live.txt/..namedfork/ads:proof
mie /root/live.txt/..namedfork/ads:proof
mfo /root/live.txt/..namedfork OFFSET
.Ed
For deleted regular files, mdxj /mount FILE_ID lists retained streams and
mgx /mount STREAM_ID OUTPUT performs checked extraction. Directory streams
use /mount/directory/..namedfork; a deleted directory can be selected with
mdxj /mount/..namedfork DIRECTORY_ID. Hard-link aliases share their owning
MFT record's stream IDs. Anonymous deleted records containing only named DATA
attributes remain discoverable with mdxj /mount 0.
.Pp
Named streams use the same resident fixup mapping, nonresident continuation,
sparse/uninitialized data and ownership checks as unnamed data. Reused attribute
list storage limits recovery to independently retained base mappings. Conflicting
duplicate stream identities cannot authorize recovery. Stream discovery uses
retained attributes and validated attribute lists; attributes removed from a
surviving record's attribute chain are not carved from its slack.
Enumerate stream IDs again after remounting a modified image.
.Pp
Checked recovery and orphan classification
are unavailable when live ownership cannot be established, including unresolved
extension references or a dirty volume requiring journal processing. Compressed and
encrypted recovery remain unsupported by the forensic callbacks, including named
streams. Ordinary path reads
retain the existing reader as a fallback where supported. These operations do
not replay LogFile or USN journal records.
.Pp
For classic HFS, use the hfs plugin. Live IDs are catalog node IDs (CNIDs);
retained candidates use bit 63 plus their catalog-relative byte offset.
The native reader scans free/unreachable catalog nodes and live-leaf slack,
retaining file and directory types. Directory ID 0 discovers candidates
without requiring surviving parent paths. Retained catalog threads can restore
cleared filenames and directory ancestry when the association is unambiguous.
Missing names use cnid-N placeholders. A thread without its file record supplies
name evidence but cannot authorize recovery of missing contents.
.Pp
Classic names use MacRoman decoding and HFS byte-order equivalence. Control
characters, percent and slash are escaped as %XX. Other legacy script encodings
remain separate work. File and resource forks include the three initial extents
and unambiguous retained overflow mappings. Checked extraction requires free
allocation bits, no live owner, no reused CNID and complete metadata ownership;
missing or reused mappings retain only the checked prefix.
.Pp
Free/allocated/orphan/bad/unknown ranges cover the allocation area described by
the MDB. They exclude boot blocks, the MDB, bitmap and trailing volume regions
outside that area. Offsets include the allocation-area origin and mount delta.
Allocation blocks can be any supported multiple of 512 bytes.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mdxj /root 0
mgx /root DELETED_ID output
mfuj /root free
mfuj /root orphan
mdj /root/live.txt/..namedfork
mie /root/live.txt/..namedfork/data-slack
mie /root/..namedfork/catalog-slack
.Ed
The virtual fork directory exposes data, rsrc, data-slack and rsrc-slack where
present. Fork slack is the storage between logical EOF and the recorded physical
EOF, including clump preallocation; ordinary file reads stop at logical EOF.
The root's catalog-slack stream concatenates unused regions of validated active
catalog leaves, excluding their offset tables. Extent enumeration preserves each
region's physical address. These bytes are evidence, not proof of deletion.
Use existing mg or mc commands for raw slack extraction and mfo with the virtual
fork directory to locate its owner. Deleted fork/slack streams use snapshot IDs
and the existing mgx command with the same ownership checks.
.Pp
Dirty or damaged metadata retains readable inspection while incomplete ownership
prevents checked recovery and orphan classification. Cached metadata describes
a read-only mounted snapshot; remount after changing the image. Bootstrapping
a self-fragmented overflow tree remains separate work.
When allocation geometry is incomplete but the ordinary reader can still mount
the volume, ordinary browsing remains available and forensic operations report
unsupported rather than inventing allocation or recovery results.
.Pp
For HFS+ and HFSX, use the hfsplus plugin. Live IDs are catalog node IDs
(CNIDs). Retained catalog candidates use bit 63 set plus their byte offset
within the catalog file, distinguishing remnants with the same CNID.
Deleted listing examines free B-tree nodes, unreachable records and live-leaf
slack. Directory ID 0 lists candidates independently of their parent paths.
These records are marked DELETED and HEURISTIC: a remnant is evidence, not
proof of deletion or historical identity. Names use HFS Unicode decomposition
and fixed HFS+ case equivalence; HFSX binary comparison remains case sensitive.
Control characters, slash and percent are escaped as %XX in paths and listings.
Unrepresentable names use synthetic cnid-N names, or an unambiguous matching
catalog thread. Thread-only remnants preserve names and directory ancestry,
but cannot supply missing file mappings. Ambiguous parent versions remain
discoverable by ID without inventing a historical path.
.Pp
File and directory hard links resolve validated private-directory backing
records. Aliases retain their own IDs and names while sharing backing forks.
Missing or conflicting targets and directory cycles retain partial inspection;
live unresolved links prevent complete ownership claims. A deleted alias that
still points to a live inode cannot authorize checked recovery of that inode.
.Pp
List a file's streams with mdj /mount/file/..namedfork. The virtual directory
contains data, rsrc and xattr:NAME entries. Open those paths with ordinary
commands, for example mc /mount/file/..namedfork/rsrc or
mie /mount/file/..namedfork/xattr:NAME. Attribute names use the same escaping;
attribute selection is exact. The data entry reuses the file ID; other streams
have mounted-snapshot IDs with bit 62 set and bit 63 clear.
Use mfo /mount/file/..namedfork OFFSET to find a stream owning a disk offset.
.Pp
For a deleted file, mdxj /mount FILE_ID lists its retained streams. Extract a
listed stream with mgx /mount STREAM_ID OUTPUT. Retained attribute versions have
distinct IDs, including versions with identical names. Deleted attributes of
live files appear in mdxj /mount/file/..namedfork. Unattached attributes appear
in mdxj /mount 0 as cnid-N:xattr:NAME. Attribute association uses retained CNIDs
and is heuristic; conflicting catalog versions prevent checked recovery.
.Pp
HFS+ data-fork mappings include overflow extents, allocation block sizes,
64-bit sizes and offsets, mount deltas and embedded HFS-wrapper offsets.
Recovery requires free allocation bits and no current owner, including resource
forks, external attributes, overflow records, special files, journal storage and
reserved volume regions. A live CNID excludes recovery of an older candidate
with that CNID. Only unambiguous retained overflow records extend deleted
forks; missing, damaged or conflicting mappings retain their readable prefix.
Cleared mappings do not establish the locations of the missing content.
Resource forks and nonresident attributes use the same checked recovery.
Inline attributes expose retained bytes in the attributes B-tree, so their
extents can be allocated metadata; they still require complete ownership and
an unambiguous identity. Attribute continuation gaps, duplicates or conflicting
versions cannot establish a complete mapping.
.Pp
Allocation maps distinguish free, allocated, bad and unknown blocks. Orphan
classification and checked recovery require complete ownership: damaged trees,
unreadable metadata, inconsistent volume flags or a pending/unverifiable journal
refuse these operations while retaining readable inspection. Journal checking
accepts a checksummed empty journal; it does not replay transactions or recover
historical journal versions. Reads never synthesize missing HFS+ file data.
Cached metadata and stream IDs describe a read-only mounted snapshot; remount
and enumerate IDs again after changing the backing image. Compressed data-fork
recovery and journal-history recovery remain separate work. Raw compression
attributes and resource forks can be extracted through the stream namespace.
.Pp
Classic HFS and HFS+/HFSX try the alternate MDB or volume header when the primary
cannot supply usable volume metadata. Candidates must have valid geometry,
bounded special-file mappings and readable B-tree headers. A usable primary
remains authoritative; a backup write counter does not establish freshness.
Alternate-based entries are HEURISTIC and a warning identifies the fallback.
Conflicting volume identities, geometry or ambiguous replacement mappings retain
inspection with PARTIAL and CONFLICT flags, but prevent checked recovery,
orphan classification and assertions that allocation blocks are free.
Readable dirty-volume evidence also prevents checked recovery.
.Pp
Backup lookup uses the containing IO region boundary or plausible primary-header
geometry, and the embedded volume boundary for an intact HFS+ wrapper. It does
not scan the disk or repair either copy. A completely erased primary requires
a known boundary; a damaged wrapper can prevent locating its embedded volume.
If the primary signature is lost, mount explicitly with m /mount hfs OFFSET or
m /mount hfsplus OFFSET because automatic filesystem identification uses the
primary signature. No new API, command or configuration setting is required.
.Pp
.Ft int
.Fn r_fs_ranges "RFS *fs" "const char *mountpoint" "ut32 flags" "RFSRangeCb cb" "void *user"
.Pp
Enumerate allocation ranges in a mounted filesystem. Flags select allocation
states such as R_FS_RANGE_FREE; unreferenced allocated space is a separate
R_FS_RANGE_ORPHAN category.
.Pp
Both iterators borrow each RFSRange for the duration of the callback, whose
false return requests a stop. Results are R_FS_WALK_COMPLETE,
R_FS_WALK_STOPPED, R_FS_WALK_ERROR or R_FS_WALK_UNSUPPORTED.
An unsupported allocation map must not be interpreted as free space.
All offsets and sizes are bytes. Physical offsets include the mount delta.
For allocation ranges, logical is the volume-relative offset.
FAT allocation maps exclude reserved sectors, FAT copies and the fixed root
region. ORPHAN requires a complete walk of live directory ownership and
selects allocated clusters without a live owner; it differs from FREE.
UNWRITTEN selects allocated ext4 blocks covered by unwritten extents of live
files; these ranges also carry R_FS_RANGE_ALLOCATED and expose the stale
physical bytes. Holes occupy no volume space, so allocation ranges never
report R_FS_RANGE_SPARSE. There is no fs.sparse search mode, and
.Ic search.in
fs modes skip any range whose physical offset is UT64_MAX.
.Pp
.Ft void
.Fn r_fs_batch "RFS *fs" "bool enable"
.Pp
Enable or disable batch mode on every mounted root. While enabled, plugins
may keep expensive state, such as live ownership maps, in RFSRoot.cache and
share it between recovery handles. Both calls drop the cached state, so the
image must not change while a batch is active. Handles opened outside a batch
always compute fresh state.
.Sh FILESYSTEM ZOOM
Use
.Ic pzg
to display the mounted filesystem as an address grid.
.Ic p=gj Ar N
and
.Ic pzgj Ar N
return the same map as JSON, with up to
.Ar N
cells. Set
.Va zoom.byte
to
.Cm g
to use this mode for
.Ic pz
and
.Ic pzj .
.Pp
.Va fs.cwd
selects the containing mount. The default directory
.Pa /
falls back to
.Pa /root
or the only mount if there is no mount at
.Pa / .
The physical range starts at the mount offset or
.Va zoom.from ,
whichever is higher, and ends at the active image size or
.Va zoom.to ,
whichever is lower. A zero
.Va zoom.to
uses the image size. The end is exclusive.
.Pp
The
.Dq filesystem
array contains cells with
.Dq addr ,
.Dq size ,
.Dq value ,
.Dq flags
and
.Dq state .
The address and size are physical bytes. Each cell ORs together the flags of
ranges that touch it. Bits 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 denote free (unallocated),
allocated, orphan, bad and unknown space. Bits 32 and 64 denote live file
extents and retained deleted-file claims. Deleted claims can overlap present
ownership or free space and do not establish recoverability. Sparse holes
have no physical location and do not set a flag.
.Pp
Values 0, 32, 64, 96, 128, 160, 192, 224 and 255 denote unmapped, free,
allocated, orphan, bad, unknown, live file, deleted claim, and live file plus
deleted claim. The highest applicable value is displayed. Cells may contain more than one flag at coarse
resolutions. Unmapped space includes sectors outside the filesystem's
allocation map. The
.Dq allocation_status
and
.Dq file_status
fields report completeness, including partial, stopped and unsupported
scans. An unsupported allocation map yields an empty array.
.Sh JOURNAL RECOVERY
The ext2 plugin also reads internal ext3 JBD and ext4 JBD2 journals through
plugin commands. It scans retained checkpointed records as well as the active
log, validates commit records and available journal checksums, applies retained revoke
records, and restores escaped block headers. Journal records are never replayed
into the mounted filesystem.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
m:ext2 journalj /root
m:ext2 journal-inodesj /root
m:ext2 journal-namesj /root 100
m:ext2 journal-namesj /root 100 13
m:ext2 journal /root 100 1234 recovered-block
m:ext2 journal-inodej /root 100 13
m:ext2 journal-inode /root 100 13 recovered-inode
m:ext2 journal-recover /root 100 13 recovered-file
m:ext2 journal-recover /root 100 13 recovered-file free
.Ed
The first command lists retained block versions with transaction sequence,
filesystem block, journal block, revocation and checksum information. The next
commands inspect or extract specific block or inode versions. The journal-inodes
command lists retained inode-table records and accepts an optional transaction
sequence filter. It
uses the current filesystem's inode-table layout to identify those records.
.Pp
The journal-names command reconstructs names from the latest retained directory
inode and directory-block versions at or before the selected committed transaction.
An optional inode number filters the results after reconstructing parent links,
so hard links can produce several names. Current directory contents are never
substituted for missing historical metadata. The text columns are transaction,
parent inode, target inode, entry/slack/conflict, and escaped path or basename.
JSON includes the directory inode version, directory block and its transaction,
name, inode, path, and the linked inode version and generation when available.
Slack entries and names older than the available inode version are not attached
to that inode version. Their inode_sequence and generation fields are null.
.Pp
All historical names are marked heuristic. Complete paths require an unambiguous
chain of normal directory entries reaching inode 2. Missing ancestors, cycles,
overlapping directory blocks and multiple directory parents leave paths unresolved;
JSON reports a null path while preserving the basename and parent inode.
Duplicate live names are marked as conflicts. Directory index payload is excluded
from name carving. Available inode and directory checksums are validated;
damaged or missing blocks leave partial evidence and a warning. Symlinks are
listed as names and are not followed when reconstructing ancestry.
.Pp
File recovery uses the latest retained block versions at or before the selected transaction;
that transaction must itself be present and committed. Missing metadata or data
stops recovery and retains the verified prefix. Symlinks are recovered as target
text, and sparse or unwritten extents read as zeros.
.Pp
File recovery defaults to journal bytes. Default ordered/writeback
journals normally contain metadata, so finding an older inode does not guarantee
that its file contents can be recovered. The optional free mode supplements
historical metadata with current disk bytes only when the inode is currently
free with the same generation, the block is free and has no live or metadata
owner, and no retained later write or revocation names the block. A revoked or
unreadable retained version is never replaced with current disk bytes.
Allocation and metadata checksums are validated where available. Unknown
allocation or missing metadata stops recovery and preserves its readable prefix.
Free-space fallback is disabled when the filesystem requires journal replay;
its current allocation metadata may be stale.
Current free-space bytes are reported as heuristic: blocks reused and freed
again may contain unrelated data even when these checks pass. The journal mode
can be specified explicitly to retain the default behavior.
Unsupported external journals and fast commits are reported explicitly.
Use m:ext2? for command help.
.Sh SHELL
.Ft bool
.Fn r_fs_shell "RFSShell *shell" "RFS *fs" "const char *root"
.Pp
Starts an interactive filesystem shell.
.Sh FILE TYPES
.Ft const RFSType *
.Fn r_fs_type_index "int i"
.Pp
Retrieves filesystem type information by index.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Common patterns used by the radare2 core and plugins:
.Pp
.Bd -literal -offset indent
// create and mount a filesystem at a given offset
RFS *fs = r_fs_new();
RFSRoot *root = r_fs_mount (fs, "fat", "/mnt/fat", 0x1000);

// open, read full contents and close
RFSFile *file = r_fs_open (fs, "/README.txt", false);
if (file) {
    r_fs_read (fs, file, 0, file->size);
    r_fs_close (fs, file);
}

// list a directory
RList *files = r_fs_dir (fs, "/");

// slurp a file into memory (single call)
RFSFile *s = r_fs_slurp (fs, "/config.bin");
if (s) {
    // use s->data and s->size
    r_fs_file_free (s);
}

